# HYPOT(3M)

## NAME

hypot - euclidean distance

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <math.h>

double hypot(double x, double y);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Hypot* returns

> sqrt(x*x + y*y),

taking precautions against unwarranted overflows.

**Note:** v7 documented *cabs* here as well, taking a **struct { double x, y; }**. It is not in
this library. The v7 source arrived wrapped in an **#if 0** and it went with it: there is no
<complex.h> on this system - **__STDC_NO_COMPLEX__** is defined - and nothing declares *cabs*.

## SEE ALSO

exp(3) for *sqrt*, floor(3), intro(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

None. The precautions are what make that true; see BESM-6 NOTES.

## BESM-6 NOTES

The care this routine takes is not a nicety here, because **an overflow is a fault and not an
infinity:** past 2**63 the program dies. The obvious spelling squares both arguments, and x*x
overflows for any |x| above 2**31.5 even when the answer is perfectly representable. Dividing
by the larger argument first keeps the ratio in [0,1] and the square in [1,2], so only the
closing multiply can reach the top of the range - and it can only reach it when the answer
itself is out of range.

The routine is otherwise v7's, unchanged.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libm.a
| lib/libm/hypot.c
